<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Advent</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Advent is a season observed in most Christian denominations as a time of waiting and preparation for the celebration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>'s birth at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> and for his return at the Second Coming. In Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> it begins on the fourth Sunday before Christmas and opens the liturgical year; its name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> adventus, &quot;coming&quot;. When the season began is not known, though it existed from about 480, and the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> of 567 ordered monks to fast every day in December until Christmas. A fast kept from St. Martin's Day on 11 November, known as St Martin's Lent, was limited to the diocese of Tours until the sixth century. Practices today include Advent calendars and wreaths.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/advent.jpg" alt="Advent" width="330" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jonathunder · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Advent2007candlelight.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It is still sung where the Tridentine liturgy of the 1962 Missal is used, and, slightly edited and divided into three parts, remains an optional hymn of the Liturgy of the Hours on All Souls' Day and in the last week before <b>Advent</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dies-irae">Dies irae</a></li><li><blockquote>Because of its prophetic theme the hymn is popular in <b>Advent</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/es-ist-ein-ros-entsprungen">Es ist ein Ros entsprungen</a></li><li><blockquote>After the 12th century <b>Advent</b> came to be treated as a penitential season like Lent, and the Gloria was left out of it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gloria-in-excelsis-deo">Gloria in excelsis Deo</a></li><li><blockquote>…Liszt, Gustav Holst and Norman Dello Joio used the carol too, Thomas Pynchon made it the choral centrepiece of an <b>Advent</b> scene in his novel Gravity's Rainbow (1973), and Mike Oldfield's instrumental arrangement of the Pearsall version reached number 4 in the UK singles chart in January 1976.</blockquote><a href="/songs/in-dulci-jubilo">In dulci jubilo</a></li><li><blockquote>It is also read in <b>Advent</b> and, among Lutherans, at the Feast of the Visitation on 2 July.</blockquote><a href="/songs/magnificat">Magnificat</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;O Come, O Come, Emmanuel&quot;, in Latin &quot;Veni, veni, Emmanuel&quot;, is a Christian hymn for <b>Advent</b>, the season before Christmas, and is often printed among Christmas carols.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-come-o-come-emmanuel">O Come, O Come, Emmanuel</a></li></ul></section>
